  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nAmong the given options, such neighborhoods exist for the point $A.$ Hence, $A$ is a local maximum .\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/assets/d19c97886c1449b414939269d9f9bf5d7ca8b418268ed5ef9b5940acaf29dbf7.png)",
